====================================================================
DRAFT BEHAVIOR vs. FINAL BEHAVIOR (WITHOUT / WITH cleanness filter)
====================================================================

Initial ADR 0015 implementation (no cleanness filter) produced
7 instances of p_2 after Phase 2:
  - {c2, c3, c4}  (discovery original)
  - {c1, c2, c3}  (clean)
  - {c3, c4, c5}  (clean)
  - {k1, k2, k3}  ×3  (three overlapping 2-chains INSIDE the 3-cycle)
  - {t1, t2, t4}  (clean)

Every consecutive edge pair of the 3-cycle — R(k1,k2)+R(k2,k3),
R(k2,k3)+R(k3,k1), R(k3,k1)+R(k1,k2) — canonicalizes to the same
2-chain form `[(1, 2), (2, 0)]` when considered in isolation. Pure
WL isomorphism has no access to the "this participant set also
participates in other edges" fact, so it cannot reject these.

These three entries had the *same participant set* `{k1, k2, k3}`
but *different edge sets*. They created three p_2 instance
identifiers for the same three nodes, and crucially broke the
ADR 0010 canonical-recovery invariant — `instance_subgraph(p_2_i_?)`
restricted to `{k1, k2, k3}` reconstructs the full 3-cycle, not
a 2-chain, so the "canonical recovers from participants" guarantee
fails for these embedded cases.

The cleanness filter fixes this. A candidate is retained iff its
participant set, restricted to data edges, induces exactly `k`
edges. For the cycle case: participants `{k1,k2,k3}` induce 3
edges (the full cycle), not 2 — rejected. For `{c1,c2,c3}`:
induces 2 (just the two chain edges) — accepted. For `{t1,t2,t4}`:
induces 2 (tree branch edges) — accepted. Canonical-recovery
invariant is preserved.

====================================================================
FINDINGS
====================================================================

1. **The fragmentation fix works.** p_2 grew from 1 to 4 instances
   on the original data alone (the three 2-chain subgraphs within
   the 5-chain plus one in the tree branch). ADR 0014's
   compound-class pipeline surfaced only 1 — the `{c2, c3, c4}`
   interior — because the chain's head-edge, middle-edge, and
   tail-edge have different compound fingerprints.

2. **The asymmetric 2-chain attaches.** After adding `{u, v, w}` and
   the T-fork's internal 2-chain `{q1, q3, q4}`, Phase 4 attaches
   both as new p_2 instances (p_2 6 → 6 through dedup; original count
   4 + 2 new = 6). This is exactly the case ADR 0014 missed.

3. **Cleanness filter is load-bearing.** Without it, the attach
   pass over-records pattern instances that are actually pieces of
   larger structures (2-chain segments of a cycle). With it, only
   "clean" instances are recorded, preserving the canonical-
   recovery invariant from ADR 0010.

4. **Two enumeration primitives coexist cleanly.**
   `compound_class_subgraphs` (ADR 0007) remains the discovery
   heuristic — cheap, surfaces candidate canonicals from unstructured
   data. `find_instances_of` (ADR 0015) is the verification primitive
   — given a known canonical, find every clean instance. Each uses
   the right strategy for its purpose.

5. **Second attach is a genuine no-op.** Phase 5 confirms: with no
   new data, the second attach produces zero additions. Dedup by
   participant set (which is unique per clean instance) filters
   every re-discovered candidate.

6. **The T-fork still doesn't get its own pattern.** `{q1, q2, q3,
   q4}` has canonical `[(2, 0), (3, 1), (3, 2)]` (a 3-edge
   asymmetric structure). Attach-only won't name it — there's no
   matching registered pattern. A discovery pass might pick it up
   in principle, but compound-class enumeration would fragment it
   the same way it fragments chains. Reaching asymmetric novel
   structures via *discovery* is a separate problem from this ADR
   (candidate for a future probe).

====================================================================
IMPLICATIONS / NEXT
====================================================================

With ADR 0015:

  - **Attach pipeline is correct** for arbitrary canonical forms,
    subject to the size budget of enumeration.
  - **Discovery still has the fragmentation limit.** Only symmetric
    structures and whatever aligns with compound fingerprints get
    picked up in the initial pass.
  - **Naming asymmetric novel structures requires a different
    approach.** Candidate: run subgraph enumeration not against a
    *specific* canonical but against a *frequency* criterion — find
    connected subgraphs of size k that canonicalize to the same
    form in ≥ 2 locations. This is subgraph-motif discovery, the
    next probe if we decide to close the discovery side too.

  - **Cleanness filter implications for future work.** It defines
    the instance-admission rule: only instances whose participants
    cleanly induce the subgraph are recorded. Future changes that
    loosen this (e.g., allow "embedded" instances with explicit
    edge storage) would need to replace the participant-only
    encoding of ADR 0010.
